From: Codex <codex@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] conhost: give double-byte characters two cells for the external shell

Purpose: lay out Japanese console text as the XP console does.
XP behavior: with a DBCS code page (932), a cell is as wide as a
half-width character; a character that the code page encodes in two bytes
takes two cells, marked COMMON_LVB_LEADING_BYTE and
COMMON_LVB_TRAILING_BYTE, and one that does not fit at the end of a line
goes to the next one.
Wine before: every cell was as wide as the font's widest character (a
FIXME), so Latin text stood one full-width cell per letter. Characters
that no key gives, such as those an IME commits, were dropped from the
console input, so Japanese could not be typed into cmd.
Change: for the console window (not a Unix terminal) with a DBCS output
code page, cells are tmAveCharWidth wide; write_char stores a two-byte
character in two cells, the line editor counts its width as two, the
window draws it once over both cells (a lone half is blank), and copying
the selection takes it once. WM_CHAR characters without a key are queued
with virtual key 0.
Evidence: in the test VM, cmd shows "Microsoft Windows 5.1.2600" and echo
output at half-width spacing, and Japanese typed with Mozc is echoed,
inserted and edited in the command line without overlapping.
Scope: only with WIN4WINE_EXTERNAL_SHELL in the console's environment.
